.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
.TH RTRASH 1 "2026-07-06" "rtrash 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rtrash \- fast freedesktop.org trash tool with an rm-compatible interface
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rtrash
[\fICOMMAND\fR] [\fIARGS\fR]...
.PP
.B rtrash
[\fIOPTION\fR]... [\fIFILE\fR]...
.PP
Multi-call names (symlinks to the same binary):
.BR trash-put ,
.BR trash ,
.BR trash-empty ,
.BR trash-list ,
.BR trash-restore ,
.BR trash-rm ,
and optionally
.B rm
(put into trash).
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rtrash
implements the freedesktop.org Trash specification on Linux: home trash
(\fI$XDG_DATA_HOME/Trash\fR) and per-volume trash directories, with atomic
.B .trashinfo
files. It provides an rm-compatible put path and a trash-cli-style suite
(list, restore, empty, selective permanent delete).
.PP
Invoked as
.B rtrash
with a known subcommand, that command runs. Invoked as
.BR rtrash
with no subcommand (or as
.BR rm ,
.BR trash ,
or
.BR trash-put ),
arguments are treated as
.B put
(move into the trash). The multi-call name
.B trash-rm
is permanent delete of matching
.I trash entries
, not the same as multi-call
.B rm
(which puts).
.PP
File names need not be UTF-8. An argument or a trashed name with other
bytes keeps them through every command. Output shows each such byte as
the Unicode replacement character.
.PP
When standard output is a terminal, names and paths in text output show
each control character (C0, DEL, and C1) and each bidirectional override
as
.BR ? ,
as
.BR ls (1)
does. A name or a
.B Path=
line in a shared trash can't move the cursor, recolor the screen, or fake
extra rows that way. A pipe, a file, and
.B \-\-json
still get the raw name. Errors about a trashed item on standard error
follow the same rule when standard error is a terminal. The browsers
drop control characters when they draw.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
When
.B rtrash
is invoked with no subcommand yet recognized:
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Output version information and exit.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS put
.B rtrash put
[\fIOPTION\fR]... [\fIFILE\fR]...
.PP
Also:
.BR trash-put ,
.BR trash ,
.BR rm
(multi-call), or bare
.B rtrash
with paths/flags.
.PP
Move
.I FILE
s into the trash. Accepts common
.BR rm (1)
flags with the same interactive/force semantics; nothing is unlinked
permanently.
.PP
A trash on another file system gets a copy, and the original goes only
once the copy is whole. The copy keeps holes in sparse files, mode, owner
where allowed, times, extended attributes, named pipes, and hard links
inside the tree. Within one btrfs file system it clones instead.
A tree with a directory this user can't empty is refused before anything
moves. Sockets can't be copied, so trashing one there fails. Restore
follows the same rules.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Ignore nonexistent files and arguments, never prompt.
.TP
.B \-i
Prompt before every removal.
.TP
.B \-I
Prompt once before removing more than three files, or when removing
recursively.
.TP
.BI \-\-interactive [=WHEN]
Prompt according to
.IR WHEN :
.BR never ,
.BR once
(\-I), or
.BR always
(\-i); without
.IR WHEN ,
prompt always.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-R ", " \-\-recursive
Remove directories and their contents (into the trash).
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-dir
Remove empty directories.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Explain what is being done.
.TP
.B \-\-one\-file\-system
Accepted for rm compatibility (no-op: entries are moved whole, never
traversed like
.BR "rm \-x" ).
.TP
.BR \-\-preserve\-root [ =all ]
Do not remove
.RB \(oq / \(cq ,
your home directory, or a directory on another device than its parent
(a mount point; renaming fails there, and a copy would drag the whole
file system into the trash).
The default;
.B =all
spells the same thing in GNU terms.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-preserve\-root
Do not treat those specially.
.PP
Put also skips paths on the safe-rm list: safe-rm's built-in system
directories (such as
.BR /usr ,
.BR /etc ,
.BR /home )
plus every path or shell pattern, one per line, in
.BR /etc/safe\-rm.conf ,
.BR /usr/local/etc/safe\-rm.conf ,
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/safe\-rm
and
.BR ~/.safe\-rm .
No flag overrides that list.
Before it trashes a directory with a git repository at its top that
has uncommitted, unpushed or unreadable work, put prints a warning;
.B rtrash undo
brings the run back.
.TP
.TP
.BI \-\-trash\-dir =PATH
Put into this FreeDesktop trash root (creates
.B files/
and
.B info/
if needed). Scripts and tests use this instead of XDG discovery.
.TP
.B \-\-home\-only
Always use the home trash (skip volume trash selection).
.TP
.B \-\-plain
Never open the put browser; require
.I FILE
operands even on a TTY.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print one JSON object on stdout:
\fItrashed\fR (one entry per moved item with \fIoriginal\fR, \fIname\fR, \fItrash_dir\fR)
and \fIfailed\fR (operands that could not be trashed).
Implies
.B \-\-plain
and replaces
.B \-v
output.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display help and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Output version information and exit.
.SS empty
.B rtrash empty
[\fIOPTION\fR]... [\fIDAYS\fR]
.PP
Also multi-call
.BR trash-empty .
.PP
Purge trashed items. With
.IR DAYS ,
only items trashed more than
.I DAYS
days ago.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Report what would be removed without removing anything; also prints
approximate reclaimable disk space.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print each removed item.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Accepted for trash-cli compatibility (emptying never prompts).
.TP
.BI \-\-trash\-dir =PATH
Empty only this trash directory (repeatable); default is the home trash
plus every mounted volume.
.TP
.B \-\-home\-only
Only the home trash (skip volume trash discovery).
.TP
.BI \-\-older\-than =DAYS
Same as positional
.I DAYS
(list/status flag style).
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-size =SIZE
Remove items until the trash directories in scope, taken together, fit in
.I SIZE
(bytes, or with a K, M, G or T suffix in powers of 1024).
With
.IR DAYS ,
the age cut runs first.
.TP
.BR \-\-prune =oldest|largest
Which items
.B \-\-max\-size
removes first (default oldest).
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit a JSON summary on stdout
(\fIdry_run\fR, \fIremoved\fR, \fIbytes\fR, \fIdays\fR, \fImax_size\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-plain
Never open the empty browser on a TTY.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display help and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Output version information and exit.
.PP
A directory the user owns but cannot list or change (mode 0311, say)
gets owner
.B rwx
back and then goes; nothing another account owns is changed.
An item that still cannot go stays, with its
.BR .trashinfo .
The rest still goes, and
.B empty
exits 1 after listing each item left and the reason.
.SS list
.B rtrash list
[\fIOPTION\fR]...
.PP
Also multi-call
.BR trash-list .
.PP
List trashed items as
.RB \(oq "DELETION-DATE ORIGINAL-PATH" \(cq,
oldest first.
.TP
.B \-\-home\-only
Only the home trash
(\fI$XDG_DATA_HOME/Trash\fR).
.TP
.BI \-\-trash\-dir =PATH
List only this trash directory (repeatable); default is the home trash
plus every mounted volume.
.TP
.B \-\-all\-users
List the trash of every account this user can read, with an owner column
after the date (and an
.B owner
field in JSON).
It reads each home trash at the default
.IR ~/.local/share/Trash ,
since another account's
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
is unknown, plus every
.I .Trash/$uid
and
.I .Trash\-$uid
on mounted volumes.
Root sees them all.
Nothing changes on disk; to restore or remove another account's items,
root pins that trash with
.BR \-\-trash\-dir .
Takes no
.B \-\-home\-only
or
.BR \-\-trash\-dir .
.TP
.BI \-\-older\-than =DAYS
Only items trashed more than
.I DAYS
days ago (same cutoff model as
.BR "empty DAYS" ).
.TP
.BI \-\-newer\-than =DAYS
Only items trashed within the last
.I DAYS
days.
.TP
.B \-\-size
Add a column with each item's disk usage: the payload plus its
.IR .trashinfo ,
as
.B status
counts it.
Directory sizes come from
.I directorysizes
when valid, and walked sizes go back into it.
.TP
.BI \-\-sort= KEY
Order rows by
.B date
(the default),
.B size
(largest first; implies
.BR \-\-size ),
.B name
(original file name) or
.B path
(original path).
Ties keep the date order.
.TP
.B \-\-reverse
Reverse the order.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit a JSON array of objects
(\fIdeletion_date\fR, \fIoriginal\fR, \fIname\fR, \fItrash_dir\fR,
\fIbatch\fR, and \fIsize\fR in bytes with
.BR \-\-size ).
The batch holds the put run id for items rtrash trashed, else null.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display help and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Output version information and exit.
.PP
Orphans (payloads in
.B files/
with no
.BR .trashinfo )
come last, dated
.B ????-??-??
and shown by their path inside the trash, with
.B \(dqorphan\(dq:true
in JSON.
An age filter leaves them out.
.SS status
.B rtrash status
[\fIOPTION\fR]...
.PP
Summarize discovered trash: item count and approximate reclaimable size
(per root and total). Uses the same disk-usage walk as
.BR "empty \-\-dry\-run" .
.TP
.B \-\-home\-only
Only the home trash
(\fI$XDG_DATA_HOME/Trash\fR).
.TP
.BI \-\-trash\-dir =PATH
Only this trash directory (repeatable).
.TP
.BI \-\-older\-than =DAYS
Only count items trashed more than
.I DAYS
days ago.
.TP
.BI \-\-newer\-than =DAYS
Only count items trashed within the last
.I DAYS
days.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit a JSON object
(\fIroots\fR, \fItotal_items\fR, \fItotal_bytes\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display help and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Output version information and exit.
.PP
Orphans count as items, noted as
.RB \(oq "(N without .trashinfo)" \(cq
per root and as
.B orphans
in JSON; an age filter leaves them out.
.PP
On Windows, status lists each drive's Recycle Bin: items, size, and the
cap from
.B MaxCapacity
in the registry with the share in use, then the total.
A drive set to skip the Recycle Bin says so.
.SS du
.B rtrash du
[\fIOPTION\fR]... [\fIPATH\fR]...
.br
.B rtrash du diff
[\fB\-n\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-apparent\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR]
.I OLD
.RI [ NEW ]
.PP
.B du diff
compares two snapshots written with
.B \-o
and lists the directories that grew or shrank most, marking those that
appeared (new) or left (gone).
Without
.IR NEW ,
it scans the roots of
.I OLD
again at the same depth.
To scan a directory named diff, write
.BR "rtrash du ./diff" .
.PP
Find what to delete. Walk each
.I PATH
(default the current directory) in parallel and list the largest
directories and files with their on-disk and apparent sizes and the age of
their newest modification. Symlinks are never followed, and a file with
more than one hard link counts once, in the sizes and in the file count,
so files plus directories match
.BR "du \-\-inodes \-s" ;
the total names the extra links. When one subdirectory accounts for
90% or more of a directory's size, only the subdirectory is listed.
The scan root itself appears only in the closing total.
A root that is a regenerable artefact (see
.BR \-\-artefacts )
has its kind named in the total, and its contents are listed, since
naming it asks to look inside.
.PP
Rows inside or containing a git repository carry a git state, found with
the
.B git
binary.
.B ignored
means an ignore rule matches the path;
.B clean
means its repository has nothing uncommitted;
.B dirty
means uncommitted changes, untracked files or a stash;
.B unpushed
means commits on a local branch that no remote has;
.B unknown
means git gave no answer.
A directory takes the riskiest state among its own repository and every
repository below it.
.B \-\-print0
leaves out dirty, unpushed and unknown rows.
Paths inside
.I .git
or a bare repository are never listed.
A bare repository is one row, and it reads
.B unpushed
when no remote has its commits.
.PP
The closing summary reports the trash on the scanned file systems.
Trashed files keep their space until
.B rtrash empty
removes them; preview with
.BR "rtrash empty \-n" ,
or keep recent items with
.BR "rtrash empty " \fIDAYS\fR.
A trash directory found during the walk is one
.B trash
row, and
.B \-\-print0
never prints it.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-top =\fIN\fR
List
.I N
directories and
.I N
files (default 20).
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-one\-file\-system
Skip directories on other file systems.
.TP
.B \-\-apparent
Rank by apparent size (file length) instead of allocated disk space.
As with coreutils
.BR du (1),
directories add to disk use but not to apparent size.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-depth =N
List only entries at most
.I N
levels below
.IR PATH .
Sizes still include everything below.
.TP
.BI \-\-threads =N
Walk with
.I N
threads. The default is one per CPU, or
.B RAYON_NUM_THREADS
when it is set. Without either, rtrash first times a few stats and opens
under the first
.IR PATH ;
when they average 10 microseconds or more, the metadata is coming off
the disk, and the walk uses three threads per CPU, up to 64. Cached, the
probe costs well under a millisecond and changes nothing. More threads
than CPUs can also help on a remote file system.
.TP
.B \-\-all\-dirs
List every directory level, including parents that one subdirectory
dominates.
.TP
.BI \-\-sort= KEY
Rank rows by
.IR KEY :
.B disk
(the default),
.B apparent
(the same as
.BR \-\-apparent ),
.B items
(files and directories below each directory, each hard-linked file once;
no file rows are listed, since every file holds one item), or
.B mtime
(newest modification first). Under
.BR mtime ,
a directory gives way to the subdirectory that contains its newest change,
so the rows name where the change happened. Snapshots do not record item
counts, so
.B \-\-file
takes every key but
.BR items .
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-count
Add an ITEMS column. The JSON rows always carry
.BR items .
.TP
.B \-\-unique
Add a UNIQUE column: the bytes of each row in extents no other file
shares, roughly what deleting it would free.
On Linux the walk asks
.B FS_IOC_FIEMAP
for every file and counts the extents not flagged shared, which btrfs
and the XFS family set for reflinks and snapshots; other Linux file
systems share nothing, so the column matches DISK.
On macOS it reads the private size APFS keeps for clones.
Elsewhere, and for files it cannot open, the column matches DISK.
The totals add a unique figure, and a closing line gives the shared
extents once each and as the files reference them.
JSON rows and roots gain
.BR unique ,
and the report gains
.BR shared .
Mapping extents costs time on every file, so the plain listing skips it.
Not with
.BR \-f ", " \-o ", " \-\-stacks " or " \-i .
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude= PATTERN
Leave out entries that match
.I PATTERN
(repeatable). Patterns use gitignore syntax: one without a
.B /
matches a name at any depth, one with a
.B /
matches the path relative to
.I PATH
(an absolute path under
.I PATH
works too), a trailing
.B /
matches directories only,
.B *
and
.B ?
stop at
.BR / ,
and
.B **
spans directories.
Excluded entries add nothing to any size or count, and an excluded
directory is never opened. The total reports how many entries were left
out.
On Unix, matching works on bytes: a pattern, ignore file or
.B \-X
file holding bytes that are not UTF-8 matches names with the same bytes.
.TP
.BR \-X ", " \-\-exclude\-from=\fIFILE\fR
Read patterns from
.IR FILE ,
one per line, with
.B #
comments and
.B !
to re-include an entry an earlier pattern left out.
.B \-
reads standard input.
.TP
.B \-\-exclude\-caches
Leave out directories below
.I PATH
that hold a
.I CACHEDIR.TAG
starting with the standard signature line.
.TP
.B \-\-gitignore
Leave out what git ignores: patterns from
.I .gitignore
files,
.I .git/info/exclude
and
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/git/ignore ,
applied only inside git repositories. A nested repository starts afresh,
as in git. A
.B core.excludesFile
set elsewhere is not read.
.TP
.B \-\-artefacts
List only regenerable build artefacts and caches.
Each one is a single row, and nothing below it is listed on its own.
The tags are
.B cargo\-target
(a directory with
.I .rustc_info.json
or a
.I target
directory with
.IR CACHEDIR.TAG ),
.B node\-modules ,
.B python\-cache
(\fI__pycache__\fR, \fI.pytest_cache\fR, \fI.mypy_cache\fR, \fI.ruff_cache\fR, \fI.hypothesis\fR),
.B python\-env
(a directory with
.IR pyvenv.cfg ,
\fI.tox\fR, \fI.nox\fR),
.B cmake\-build
(a directory with
.IR CMakeCache.txt ),
.B gradle\-cache ,
.B js\-build\-cache
(\fI.next\fR, \fI.nuxt\fR, \fI.parcel\-cache\fR, \fI.turbo\fR, \fI.svelte\-kit\fR),
.B zig\-cache ,
.B pixi\-env ,
.B direnv ,
and
.B cache
for any other directory with a
.I CACHEDIR.TAG
file.
The same tags appear in the NOTE column without this flag.
.TP
.B \-\-dirs
Show the directory table alone.
.TP
.B \-\-files
Show the file table alone.
.TP
.BI \-\-older\-than =DAYS
Only list entries whose newest modification is more than
.I DAYS
days old. A directory uses the newest file anywhere below it.
.TP
.BI \-\-newer\-than =DAYS
Only list entries modified within the last
.I DAYS
days.
.TP
.B \-\-home\-only
Consider only the home trash for the trash summary and
.B trash
rows.
.TP
.BI \-\-trash\-dir =PATH
Consider only this trash directory (repeatable).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-git
Do not ask git about listed paths.
.TP
.B \-\-include\-dirty
With
.BR \-\-print0 ,
also print rows whose git state is dirty, unpushed or unknown.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit one JSON object
(\fIroots\fR, \fIdirs\fR, \fIfiles\fR, \fIunreadable\fR); each root has
\fIpath\fR, \fIdisk\fR, \fIapparent\fR, \fIfiles\fR, \fIlinks\fR (extra hard links),
\fIdirs\fR and \fIartefact\fR; each listed entry has
\fIpath\fR, \fItype\fR, \fIdisk\fR, \fIapparent\fR, \fImtime\fR, \fIartefact\fR
(a tag or null), \fIgit\fR and \fIgit_repo\fR (state and deciding repository, or null).
A scan stopped by Ctrl-C adds \fIpartial\fR: true.
.TP
.B \-\-print0
Print the listed paths separated by null bytes, for
.BR "xargs \-0 rtrash put \-rf \-\-" .
.TP
.B \-\-pick
Open the put browser on the listed rows (directories, then files).
Mark rows and press Enter to trash them after a confirmation; rows with
dirty, unpushed or unknown git state show
.B !
and the confirmation counts them.
Needs a terminal on stdin and the
.B tui
feature.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output =\fIFILE\fR
Write a snapshot of every directory total, down to
.BR \-\-max\-depth ,
to
.I FILE
.RB ( \- " for stdout)"
instead of listing.
Snapshots hold one record per line with percent-encoded paths, so grep and
text diff tools read them too.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-file =\fIFILE\fR
List the largest directories from a snapshot instead of scanning.
A snapshot can be stale, so it cannot drive
.BR \-\-print0 ,
.BR \-\-pick " or " \-\-interactive .
.TP
.B \-\-stacks
Print folded stacks instead of listing: one line per directory, its path
from the scanned root as frames joined by
.BR ; ,
then the bytes it uses outside the directories below it.
Flame graph tools draw these lines:
.BR flamegraph.pl ,
.BR inferno\-flamegraph ,
or speedscope.
The weight follows disk use, or
.B \-\-apparent
size; other sort keys exit 2.
A
.B ;
or control character in a name prints as
.BR _ .
Works on a snapshot with
.BR \-f .
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interactive
Explore one
.I PATH
a directory at a time, in the
.B \-\-sort
order (largest first by default), with a size bar per row.
Right, l or Enter opens a directory; left, h or Backspace goes up.
.B d
moves the cursor row (or the marked rows) to the trash after a
confirmation,
.B u
restores the last deletion, and
.B r
rescans the current directory.
Sizes of the parent directories follow each change without a rescan.
Each directory opens at once: its entries show while the scan runs in the
background, files with their sizes and directories with
.B …
until their totals come in, and the title counts files and bytes so far.
Moving, opening another directory and going up all work meanwhile;
trashing waits until the view has its sizes.
.B q
or Ctrl-C while the current directory scans stops that scan and keeps
what it counted: the title shows
.BR (partial) ,
directories it never reached keep their placeholder rows, and
.B r
rescans in full.
Pressed again, or with no scan running, the key quits.
.BR s ", " n ", " C " and " M
order the rows by size, name, item count and newest change, in every
directory; the key of the current order reverses it.
The window title names the order.
.B S
switches sizes, bars and totals between disk use and apparent size,
and back; a size order follows the switch.
.B i
opens the details of the cursor row: full path, kind, disk use and its
share of the directory, apparent size, item count, newest change, git
state and, on Unix, mode, owner, group and link count.
Any key closes them.
.B /
filters the current directory by name as you type: a part of the name,
or a glob when the text holds
.BR * ", " ? " or " [ ,
with case ignored.
Enter keeps the filter, and Esc, or
.B q
in the list, clears it.
Marking all, trashing and the title count cover only the rows shown,
and opening or leaving the directory ends the filter.
Rows show the artefact kind and git state; risky git state shows
.BR ! .
Trash directories stay closed.
Needs a terminal on stdin and the
.B tui
feature.
On Windows,
.B d
uses the Recycle Bin and
.B u
takes the newest item with that path back out.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-delete
With
.BR \-i ,
browse only:
.B d
and
.B u
show a note instead of acting, and the title reads
.BR "(read\-only)" .
Safe on a shared server or a tree you only want to measure.
Without
.B \-i
it exits 2, since no other mode deletes.
The
.B no_delete
config key turns it on for every
.BR \-i .
.TP
.B \-\-si
Show sizes in powers of 1000 (kB, MB, GB, TB) instead of 1024 (KiB,
MiB, GiB), in the listing, the totals, the trash line and the explorer.
JSON, snapshots and stacks stay in bytes.
The
.B si
config key makes it the default.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display help and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Output version information and exit.
.SS restore
.B rtrash restore
[\fIOPTION\fR]... [\fIPATH\fR]
.PP
Also multi-call
.BR trash-restore .
.PP
Restore a trashed item to its original location. With
.IR PATH ,
restore the item originally at
.IR PATH
(exact original-path match).
With
.BI \-\-name= NAME ,
restore the item whose file name, or whose name inside the trash, is
.IR NAME .
Without
.IR PATH ,
on a TTY open the interactive restore browser (filter, navigate, restore),
on Windows as well, where it shows the Recycle Bin;
without a TTY (or with
.BR \-\-plain )
print a numbered list and read a selection from stdin: indices, ranges
and commas such as
.BR 0\-2,5 ,
or
.BR all .
Pass
.B \-\-cwd\-only
to limit bare restore to originals under the current directory.
A single match restores directly; multiple matches require a selection.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Overwrite an existing file at the destination (same as
.BR \-\-conflict=overwrite ).
.TP
.BI \-\-conflict= POLICY
What to do when the destination exists:
.B refuse
(the default; the item stays in the trash and the run fails),
.BR overwrite ,
.B rename
(restore next to it as
.IR STEM .1. EXT ,
counting up)
or
.B skip
(the item stays in the trash; the run still succeeds).
.TP
.BI \-\-to= DIR
Restore into the existing directory
.I DIR
instead of the original location, keeping each item's file name.
.TP
.B \-\-cwd\-only
Without PATH, only items trashed from under the current directory.
.TP
.B \-\-plain
Force numbered line selection even on a TTY (skip the TUI browser).
.TP
.B \-\-newest\-first
List the most recently trashed item first (default in the interactive browser).
.TP
.B \-\-oldest\-first
Order from oldest to newest (default for the numbered list, so the newest row prints just above the prompt).
.TP
.BI \-\-name= NAME
Match items by original file name or by name inside the trash, instead of by
.IR PATH .
.TP
.BI \-\-glob= PATTERN
Match items by shell pattern: against the original path when
.I PATTERN
contains a slash, else against the original file name or the name inside
the trash.
.TP
.BI \-\-regex= REGEX
Match items whose original path contains a match for
.IR REGEX .
Give only one of
.BR \-\-name ,
.B \-\-glob
and
.BR \-\-regex .
.TP
.B \-\-latest
When several items match, restore the most recently trashed one without asking.
.TP
.B \-\-all
When several items match, restore every one without asking.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print
.B {"restored": [...], "failed": N, "skipped": N}
on stdout; each restored item also names its
.IR restored_to . Implies
.BR \-\-plain ;
the numbered table, if any, goes to stderr.
.TP
.B \-\-home\-only
Only the home trash (skip volume trash).
.TP
.BI \-\-trash\-dir =PATH
Only consider this trash directory (repeatable).
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display help and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Output version information and exit.
.SS undo
.B rtrash undo
[\fIOPTION\fR]... [\fIBATCH\fR]
.PP
Restore every item one put run trashed, each to its original location.
Each put run writes one batch id into its
.B .trashinfo
files, under the extra key
.BR X-Rtrash-Batch .
Without
.IR BATCH ,
undo the most recent run in scope.
Items trashed by other tools carry no batch id and never match.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-list
List put runs, newest first: id, date, item count and paths.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Show what would come back and change nothing.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Overwrite existing files at the original locations.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print
.RI { batch ", " restored ", " failed }
as JSON on stdout; with
.BR \-\-list ,
an array of runs.
.TP
.B \-\-home\-only
Only the home trash (skip volume trash).
.TP
.BI \-\-trash\-dir =PATH
Only consider this trash directory (repeatable).
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display help and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Output version information and exit.
.PP
Exit status 0 means every item came back; 1 means nothing matched or an item failed.
.SS finder
.B rtrash finder put
[\fB\-v\fR] [\fB\-f\fR] \fIFILE\fR...
.br
.B rtrash finder list
[\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-trash\-dir=\fIDIR\fR]
.br
.B rtrash finder restore
[\fIOPTION\fR]... \fINAME\fR|\fIORIGINAL\fR...
.PP
Use the macOS Finder Trash, with Put Back.
.B put
asks Finder (through
.BR osascript )
to move each
.I FILE
to the Trash, so Finder records the original location and its own Put
Back works; macOS only, and symbolic links get refused.
.B list
prints each item as
.RB \(oq "NAME \-> ORIGINAL\-PATH" \(cq,
reading Finder's Put Back records
.RI ( ptbL
and
.IR ptbN )
from the trash's
.IR .DS_Store ,
with
.B ?
for an item without one.
.B restore
moves items back, matched by their name inside the trash or by original
path, and leaves
.I .DS_Store
for Finder to tidy.
List and restore read files only, so they also run on Linux against a
copied trash.
Experimental: tested against crafted
.I .DS_Store
files, not yet against Finder.
.TP
.BI \-\-trash\-dir= DIR
Use this Finder trash (default
.IR ~/.Trash ).
For a volume trash such as
.IR /Volumes/X/.Trashes/501 ,
Put Back paths count from the volume root above
.IR .Trashes .
.TP
.B \-\-all
Restore every item that has a Put Back record.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Put: ignore missing files.
Restore: overwrite an existing destination (same as
.BR \-\-conflict=overwrite ).
.TP
.BI \-\-conflict= POLICY
.BR refuse " (default), " overwrite ,
.B rename
or
.BR skip ,
as for
.BR restore .
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Put: name each trashed file.
.TP
.B \-\-json
List: a JSON array of
.RI { name ", " original }.
Restore:
.BR {"restored": [...], "failed": N, "skipped": N} .
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display help and exit.
.SS config
.B rtrash config
[\fB\-\-path\fR | \fB\-\-default\fR]
.PP
Show the config file and the settings it makes, with a warning for each
line that cannot be read; the exit status is 1 when there is one.
Settings rank below environment variables and command-line options.
The file holds TOML tables:
.RS
.nf
[du]
top = 20                  # \-\-top
sort = "disk"             # \-\-sort: disk, apparent, items or mtime
count = false             # \-\-count
one_file_system = false   # \-x
threads = 8               # \-\-threads
git = true                # false = \-\-no\-git
exclude = ["*.iso"]       # \-\-exclude, before any given on the command line
exclude_caches = false    # \-\-exclude\-caches
gitignore = false         # \-\-gitignore
no_delete = false         # \-\-no\-delete for every \-i
si = false                # \-\-si

[list]
sort = "date"             # \-\-sort: date, size, name or path
size = false              # \-\-size
reverse = false           # \-\-reverse

[policy]
max_size = "50G"          # RTRASH_MAX_SIZE
prune = "oldest"          # RTRASH_PRUNE: oldest or largest

[protect]
paths = ["/srv/data", "/home/*/keep"]
.fi
.RE
.PP
Only these keys are read: strings, integers, booleans and arrays of
strings. An unknown key or a value of the wrong type gets a warning and
is skipped; the rest of the file still applies.
.B protect.paths
works like the safe-rm lists:
.B put
refuses a path that matches an entry exactly or as a shell pattern.
A config file cannot be undone by an option, so
.B RTRASH_CONFIG=
(empty) runs without it.
.TP
.B \-\-path
Print where the config file is looked for.
.TP
.B \-\-default
Print a config file with every setting commented out.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display help and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Output version information and exit.
.SS setup
.B rtrash setup
[\fIOPTION\fR]...
.PP
Install multi-call symlinks, bash/zsh completions, and this man page under a
user prefix (default
.IR ~/.local ,
or
.B $PREFIX
if set). Assets are
.B embedded in the binary
so a
.B cargo install
user does not need a source checkout. Preferred post-install step:
.BR "rtrash setup" .
.TP
.BI \-\-prefix =DIR
Install root (default:
.IR ~/.local ).
.TP
.BI \-\-bin\-dir =DIR
Override the directory for multi-call symlinks (default:
.IR PREFIX/bin ).
.TP
.B \-\-with\-rm
Also link
.B rm
→ rtrash (put into trash).
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Print actions without writing.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Replace existing links/files (use after upgrades).
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print each path written.
.SS completions
.B rtrash completions
.RB { bash | zsh }
.PP
Print the embedded completion script to stdout (packagers / custom paths).
.SS man
.B rtrash man
.PP
Print the embedded man page source to stdout.
.SS rm (permanent delete from trash)
.B rtrash rm
[\fIOPTION\fR]...
.I PATTERN
...
.PP
Also multi-call
.BR trash-rm .
.PP
Permanently remove trashed items whose original path or basename matches
a
.I PATTERN
(shell-style glob; quote globs from the shell). Matching entries are
deleted from the trash
(\fBfiles/\fR and
.BR .trashinfo ),
not restored.
An orphan (no
.BR .trashinfo )
matches by its name in
.B files/
or its path there.
An absolute
.I PATTERN
also matches with symlinks resolved in its directory part (up to the
first glob), the form
.B put
records, so a path typed through a symlinked directory still matches.
.PP
Note: multi-call name
.B rm
means
.I put into trash
(safe). This command (subcommand
.B rtrash rm
or multi-call
.BR trash-rm )
permanently deletes matching
.I trash
entries — not a synonym for put.
.TP
.BI \-\-trash\-dir =PATH
Only consider this trash directory (repeatable).
.TP
.B \-\-home\-only
Only the home trash (skip volume trash).
.TP
.BI \-\-older\-than =DAYS
Only match items trashed more than
.I DAYS
days ago.
.TP
.BI \-\-newer\-than =DAYS
Only match items trashed within the last
.I DAYS
days.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
List matches and reclaimable size; do not delete.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Allow mass patterns that match everything (e.g.
.RB \(oq * \(cq).
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print each permanently removed original path.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit a JSON summary with a
.B matches
array.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display help and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Output version information and exit.
.PP
Examples:
.nf
.RS
rtrash rm \(aq*.o\(aq
rtrash rm \-n \(aq*.o\(aq
trash-rm foo
.RE
.fi
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Base for the home trash directory
(\fI$XDG_DATA_HOME/Trash\fR). Defaults under
.I ~/.local/share
when unset.
.TP
.B HOME
Used when resolving the default data home.
.TP
.B RTRASH_CONFIG
The config file to read instead of
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/rtrash/config.toml .
Empty turns the config file off.
.TP
.B RTRASH_MAX_SIZE
When set to a size such as 10G, each put afterwards prunes every trash
directory it used back under that size, removing older items first.
Items of the same put run never go. It overrides
.B policy.max_size
in the config file, and an empty value turns the prune off.
.TP
.B RTRASH_PRUNE
Set to
.B largest
to make that prune remove the largest items first (default
.BR oldest ).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/Trash
Home trash:
.BR files/ ,
.BR info/ ,
.BR directorysizes ,
and optional lock file.
Cache sizes count allocated blocks as
.B du \-B1
does.
.B status
and
.B "empty \-\-dry\-run"
write walked sizes back.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/rtrash/config.toml
Config file (see
.BR config );
.I ~/.config/rtrash/config.toml
when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset, and
.I %APPDATA%\(rsrtrash\(rsconfig.toml
on Windows.
.TP
.IR /etc/safe\-rm.conf ", " ~/.safe\-rm
safe-rm protected paths that put skips (see
.BR put ).
.TP
.IR $top /.Trash/ $uid
or
.IR $top /.Trash\- $uid
Per-volume trash on other mounts (see the Trash specification).
A
.I $top/.Trash
that exists as a symlink or a plain file, or lacks the sticky bit,
gets a warning, and put falls back to
.IR $top /.Trash\- $uid .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Failure (e.g. could not trash or purge an item).
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.TP
.B 130
.B du
stopped by Ctrl-C.
The text listing and
.B \-\-json
still show what was scanned, marked partial;
.BR \-\-print0 ,
.BR \-\-pick ,
.B \-\-output
and
.B \-\-stacks
print nothing, since a partial list would pass for a whole one.
A second Ctrl-C ends the process at once.
.PP
Put mode mirrors common
.BR rm (1)
exit conventions.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rm (1),
Trash specification:
.UR https://specifications.freedesktop.org/trash-spec/trashspec-latest.html
.UE
.PP
Shell completions ship in the source tree under
.I completions/
(bash and zsh). See the project README for install paths.
